    seoTitle: 'Check JSON data against a real contract',
    seoIntro: 'JSON syntax validation answers only one question: can a parser read this document? JSON Schema validation answers the more useful question: does this document have the shape, types and values that an application expects? Paste both documents here to inspect a payload before a deployment, compare a fixture with an API contract, or troubleshoot a configuration file. Results stay in the browser and each failure is presented with a JSON Pointer style data path, the schema keyword and a plain language message.',
    seoStructure: 'A schema can require fields such as id or email, restrict a value to a type, set minimum and maximum values, limit array length, enforce unique items, match a regular expression, or allow only a controlled enum. Object rules can reject unexpected properties, while allOf, anyOf, oneOf and not express alternatives. Local $ref links let you define a reusable shape under $defs or definitions and apply it in several places.',
    seoTip: 'Fix the most specific path first. A missing required field can make several downstream checks noisy, and an incorrect parent type can hide the useful child errors. Keep schemas small while designing them, then add descriptions and examples beside the contract in your project.',
    seoDiagnostic: 'This is a focused client-side validator, not a complete replacement for the validator used by your production runtime. It does not fetch remote references or execute custom format vocabularies. Treat the result as a fast feedback loop and keep an authoritative test in the same environment as your API or application.',
